From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 02:29:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] northbridge/intel/haswell: Reset the setup options on RTC
 power loss

With DRIVERS_EFI_RESET_OPTIONS_ON_RTC_FAILURE, a CMOS clear also resets
the setup options, which live in SMMSTORE, but only Cannon Lake romstage
called efi_option_reset_all(). Haswell romstage now calls it right
after early_pch_init() when rtc_failure() reports RTC_PWR_STS, before
haswell_early_initialization(), whose VT-d, PEG and graphics settings
are the first options read after the console. The S3 check comes later
and clears SLP_TYP, so the gate uses platform_is_resuming(), which only
reads it, through the PMBASE that early_pch_init() has just set. RTCRST#
clears SLP_TYP, so that gate only keeps a resume from writing the flash.

Unlike on Cannon Lake, nothing is cleared here: sb_rtc_init() in
ramstage already logs the ELOG RTC reset event, clears RTC_PWR_STS and
resets the CMOS at every boot that has it. A reset by mrc.bin or the
native RAM init before that point repeats the deletion, which then
finds nothing more, and the log of that boot says 0 options. The cost
is that a store the deletion refuses is not tried again; Load Defaults
in the setup still works.

early_pch_init() halts the TCO timer, and the bit stays set up to
ramstage, so a deletion that hung would hang every boot after it. POST
code 0x37 comes before the deletion and 0x38 after it, both unused on
this platform: with CMOS_POST, a boot whose previous boot stopped at
0x37 skips the deletion, and ramstage clears the bit as usual. On
Haswell the option therefore depends on CMOS_POST. That mark is in the
CMOS as well: after a hang there, the machine has to be turned off with
the power button while AC or a battery stays attached, because with a
dead coin cell, removing every supply loses the mark and the next boot
deletes again. The help says so. The pre-CBMEM console keeps only its
last 16 KiB, less than a training boot, so the outcome is printed again
once CBMEM is up.

The Kconfig help no longer names one board. It covers laptops (with a
dead coin cell, removing or draining the battery without AC is enough,
and so is turning BIOS Lock off that way), the partial reset that an
SPI error can leave, and how each platform clears the RTC failure. The
option stays default n, so the other Haswell boards are unchanged.

TEST=Host syntax check of romstage.c with the configs of the W541 ci-150
builds, mrc and nri, with the option off and on (gcc -m32 -Werror and
the coreboot warning flags). Objects built the same way reference
efi_option_reset_all(), rtc_failure(), platform_is_resuming() and
cmos_post_previous_boot() only with the option on. coreboot's kconfig
(conf --olddefconfig) on a small Kconfig with this file: the option
stays with Haswell and CMOS_POST and on other platforms, and is dropped
on Haswell without CMOS_POST. Not built with the coreboot toolchain. The
W541 defconfigs keep the option off for now. For the W541 hardware test,
first check whether software can set GEN_PMCON_3 bit 2 (00:1f.0 0xa4:
write, read back, reboot). Then, with the option on, after a CMOS clear
(AC, battery and coin cell out): "Setup options: N reset to defaults
(RTC power lost)" or "RTC power lost: setup options reset to defaults",
"RTC: failed = 0x1", an ELOG RTC Reset event and the setup at its
defaults; no reset at the next boot or on an S3 resume. The test notes
must give the way out of a deletion that hangs (stuck at POST 0x37):
hold the power button for 4 s with AC or the battery still attached, and
the next boot logs "Setup options: not reset, last boot hung there";
removing every supply would repeat the deletion.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/drivers/efi/Kconfig                  | 53 ++++++++++------
 src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c | 77 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 112 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/drivers/efi/Kconfig b/src/drivers/efi/Kconfig
index a449e06aca..c6a552ea61 100644
--- a/src/drivers/efi/Kconfig
+++ b/src/drivers/efi/Kconfig
@@ -10,33 +10,50 @@ config DRIVERS_EFI_VARIABLE_STORE
 config DRIVERS_EFI_RESET_OPTIONS_ON_RTC_FAILURE
 	bool "Reset the setup options when the RTC loses power"
 	depends on USE_UEFI_VARIABLE_STORE
+	# The Haswell trigger skips a deletion that hung with the CMOS POST code
+	depends on CMOS_POST || !NORTHBRIDGE_INTEL_HASWELL
 	default n
 	help
 	  The setup options are EFI variables in SMMSTORE, so a CMOS clear
 	  (the coin cell removed, or a clear-CMOS jumper) leaves them as
 	  they are. With this option, when the RTC has lost power, romstage
 	  marks every variable of the coreboot options GUID as deleted
-	  before the memory settings are read: that boot runs with the
-	  defaults, and the payload writes them back. The console options
-	  are read earlier, so the console of that boot up to ramstage
-	  keeps the old debug level and serial setting.
-
-	  Every option returns to its default, the security ones too: on
-	  the Lenovo M720q/M920q/M920x, BIOS Lock goes back to its default
-	  (off without BOOTMEDIA_SMM_BWP), the Intel ME to enabled and
+	  before it reads the first option after its console: that boot
+	  runs with the defaults, and the payload writes them back. The
+	  console options are read earlier, so the console of that boot up
+	  to ramstage keeps the old debug level and serial setting.
+
+	  Every option returns to its default, the security ones too: BIOS
+	  Lock goes back to its default (off without BOOTMEDIA_SMM_BWP),
 	  Firmware protection to on, so turn it off again before writing
-	  the flash with flashrom. The Secure Boot keys, passwords, boot
-	  entries and the other variables of the payload are kept, but the
-	  entries that a setup switch removes (such as iPXE and CSMWrap)
-	  come back if it was off. With a dead coin cell, every loss of AC
-	  power resets the options.
+	  the flash with flashrom, and the Intel ME to enabled on the boards
+	  with that option. The Secure Boot keys, passwords, boot entries
+	  and the other variables of the payload are kept, but the entries
+	  that a setup switch removes (such as iPXE and CSMWrap) come back
+	  if it was off.
+
+	  The RTC loses power only when the coin cell and every other
+	  supply of the RTC well are gone. With a dead coin cell, that is
+	  every loss of AC power on a desktop, and on a laptop every time
+	  the main battery (and a second one) is removed or runs flat while
+	  no AC adapter is attached: anyone who can do that can turn BIOS
+	  Lock off.
 
 	  A malformed store, or one with an interrupted write that the
-	  payload will redo, is not changed. coreboot then leaves the RTC
-	  failure set, for the next boot to try again, but FSP-M may clear
-	  it on its own, and then that CMOS clear does not reset them.
-
-	  Only soc/intel/cannonlake implements it.
+	  payload will redo, is not changed, and an SPI error can leave a
+	  part of the options reset. On Cannon Lake, coreboot then leaves
+	  the RTC failure set, for the next boot to try again, but FSP-M may
+	  clear it on its own. On Haswell with Lynx Point, ramstage clears
+	  it at every boot, so the next boot does not try again. There the
+	  option needs CMOS_POST: a boot whose previous boot hung in the
+	  deletion skips it. That mark is in the CMOS as well, so after such
+	  a hang turn the machine off with the power button (4 s) and keep
+	  the AC adapter or the battery attached: with a dead coin cell,
+	  removing every supply loses the mark and the next boot deletes
+	  again. Load Defaults (F9) and saving (F10) in the setup reset the
+	  options in every case.
+
+	  soc/intel/cannonlake and northbridge/intel/haswell implement it.
 
 config DRIVERS_EFI_FW_INFO
 	bool "Expose firmware version in a EFI-friendly form"
diff --git a/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c b/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
index 4c70400016..f56aeddae1 100644
--- a/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
+++ b/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
@@ -1,19 +1,91 @@
 /* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only */
 
 #include <arch/romstage.h>
+#include <bootmode.h>
 #include <console/console.h>
 #include <cpu/intel/haswell/haswell.h>
 #include <device/mmio.h>
 #include <device/pci_ops.h>
+#include <drivers/efi/option.h>
 #include <elog.h>
+#include <post.h>
 #include <romstage_handoff.h>
 #include <security/intel/txt/txt.h>
 #include <security/intel/txt/txt_register.h>
 #include <northbridge/intel/haswell/haswell.h>
 #include <northbridge/intel/haswell/raminit.h>
 #include <southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.h>
+#include <southbridge/intel/common/rtc.h>
 #include <southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h>
 
+/* POST codes before and after the deletion of the setup options */
+#define POST_OPTIONS_RESET	0x37
+#define POST_OPTIONS_RESET_DONE	0x38
+
+/* What reset_options_on_rtc_failure() did, logged again once CBMEM is up */
+static enum {
+	OPTIONS_KEPT,
+	OPTIONS_RESET,
+	OPTIONS_NOT_RESET,
+	OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED,
+} options_reset;
+
+/*
+ * The RTC lost power (a CMOS clear): reset the setup options too, which live in
+ * SMMSTORE and not in the CMOS. This runs before haswell_early_initialization(),
+ * which reads the first option after the console (vtd), and so before the S3
+ * check, which clears SLP_TYP: platform_is_resuming() only reads it, with the
+ * PMBASE that early_pch_init() set. RTCRST# clears SLP_TYP, so a boot with an RTC
+ * failure is never a resume, but a resume must not write the flash.
+ *
+ * RTC_PWR_STS is left as it is: sb_rtc_init() in ramstage logs the ELOG event,
+ * clears it and resets the CMOS, as before. A reset before ramstage repeats the
+ * deletion, which then finds nothing more; a refused store is not tried again.
+ */
+static void reset_options_on_rtc_failure(void)
+{
+	if (!CONFIG(DRIVERS_EFI_RESET_OPTIONS_ON_RTC_FAILURE) || !rtc_failure() ||
+	    platform_is_resuming())
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * early_pch_init() halted the TCO timer, and the bit stays set up to
+	 * ramstage: a deletion that hangs would hang every boot that follows.
+	 * CMOS_POST, which Kconfig requires here, has the last code of the
+	 * previous boot: skip the deletion when that boot stopped in it. The
+	 * mark is in the CMOS too: a dead coin cell with every supply removed
+	 * loses it, a power-button shutdown with AC or battery keeps it.
+	 */
+	if (CONFIG(CMOS_POST)) {
+		u32 extra;
+		u8 code;
+
+		cmos_post_previous_boot(&code, &extra);
+		if (code == POST_OPTIONS_RESET) {
+			printk(BIOS_ERR, "Setup options: not reset, last boot hung there\n");
+			options_reset = OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED;
+			return;
+		}
+	}
+
+	post_code(POST_OPTIONS_RESET);
+	if (efi_option_reset_all() == CB_SUCCESS)
+		options_reset = OPTIONS_RESET;
+	else
+		options_reset = OPTIONS_NOT_RESET;
+	post_code(POST_OPTIONS_RESET_DONE);
+}
+
+/* The pre-CBMEM console keeps only its last 16 KiB, less than a training boot */
+static void log_options_reset(void)
+{
+	if (options_reset == OPTIONS_RESET)
+		printk(BIOS_INFO, "RTC power lost: setup options reset to defaults\n");
+	else if (options_reset != OPTIONS_KEPT)
+		printk(BIOS_ERR, "RTC power lost: setup options not reset%s\n",
+		       options_reset == OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED ? " (last boot hung there)" : "");
+}
+
 void __weak mb_late_romstage_setup(void)
 {
 }
@@ -31,6 +103,9 @@ void mainboard_romstage_entry(void)
 
 	post_code(0x31);
 
+	/* Before the first option read after the console */
+	reset_options_on_rtc_failure();
+
 	/* Perform some early chipset initialization required
 	 * before RAM initialization can work
 	 */
@@ -90,6 +165,8 @@ void mainboard_romstage_entry(void)
 
 	romstage_handoff_init(s3resume);
 
+	log_options_reset();
+
 	post_code(0x3d);
 
 	mb_late_romstage_setup();
-- 
2.47.3

